  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Question · Biology</p>
    <h1>What Colour Are Raven Eggs?</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Raven eggs are <strong>pale blue-green to greenish-grey</strong>, heavily blotched with brown, grey, and olive-brown markings, usually concentrated at the larger end. Common Raven eggs measure approximately 48–52 mm × 33–35 mm. Clutch size is typically 3–7 eggs (4–6 most common). Incubation lasts approximately 20–21 days, primarily by the female.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Egg Description</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Common Raven egg characteristics:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Ground colour:</strong> pale blue-green, greenish, or greenish-grey. The blue-green is variable — some eggs are quite vivid; others are duller and more olive.</li>
      <li><strong>Markings:</strong> blotches, spots, and streaks of brown, reddish-brown, grey, and olive-brown. Markings are typically concentrated at the larger (blunt) end, though some eggs have more evenly distributed blotching.</li>
      <li><strong>Size:</strong> approximately 48–52 mm long × 33–35 mm wide in Common Ravens — slightly larger than a large chicken egg in length but narrower.</li>
      <li><strong>Shape:</strong> oval, slightly asymmetrical — the blunt end noticeably broader than the pointed end.</li>
      <li><strong>Texture:</strong> shell slightly glossy when fresh.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Variation Across Species</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> All raven species produce eggs with the same basic appearance — blue-green ground colour with brown/grey blotching — consistent with the broader corvid egg pattern. Specific measurements vary:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Common Raven (<em>C. corax</em>):</strong> ~48–52 × 33–35 mm.</li>
      <li><strong>Chihuahuan Raven (<em>C. cryptoleucus</em>):</strong> slightly smaller — ~43–49 × 30–33 mm.</li>
      <li><strong>Australian Raven (<em>C. coronoides</em>):</strong> ~44–50 × 30–34 mm.</li>
      <li><strong>African/Middle Eastern species (White-necked, Thick-billed, Fan-tailed, Brown-necked):</strong> similar size range, same colour pattern.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Why Are Raven Eggs Blue-Green?</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Blue-green egg colour in corvids and other birds is produced by biliverdin — a bile pigment deposited in the eggshell during formation. The blotches of brown and grey are produced by protoporphyrin and biliverdin deposited at a later stage or in different concentrations. The specific function of this colour pattern in ravens is debated; possible functions include camouflage of the eggs in the nest against visual predators, stimulation of incubation behaviour in the parents, or signalling of egg quality. In corvids that nest in concealed or high cliff sites, egg camouflage may be less critical than in ground-nesting species.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

